# Generate, customize and test a Venus OS service

The validated generator is `scripts/render_template.py` in the
[template repository](https://github.com/4alvit/dbus-service-template). Use Python
3.12 and the hash-locked template requirements described by that repository's
README. Direct Copier invocation is not the tested generation path.

## Answers and generated identity

Copy the template's `.copier-answers.yml.example` and set each desired scalar
explicitly: project name/description, package slug, Python module, class prefix,
D-Bus name, device type, instance, broker, topic prefix and feature flags.
The accepted format is one `key: value` per line with simple booleans, integers
and optionally quoted strings; nested YAML, block strings and substitutions are
not supported. Names do not automatically recompute other fields.

Python module/class names must be ASCII identifiers; package/owner names use
letters, digits, hyphens and underscores. D-Bus names are dotted identifiers.
Strings cannot contain control characters or double-quote, backslash, backtick,
dollar or brace source delimiters. Instance is 0..255, MQTT port 1..65535.
Only the shipped MIT license and Python 3.12 are supported. Application version
is a three-part number, optionally followed by a Python a/b/rc number.

This generated project is **{{ project_name }}**, module `{{ module_name }}`,
version `{{ version }}`, device type `{{ device_type }}`. Its version and security
assessment are independent of the source generator.

## Local development

Create an isolated Python 3.12 environment in the generated project, install with
`python -m pip install -e '.[dev]'`, then run `python -m pytest tests/` and
`python -m ruff check src/`. Tests use mocked D-Bus objects and no broker connection.
A CLI invocation may start service/network activity; inspect its configuration
before running it. Review generated workflows before enabling them.

Extend `src/{{ module_name }}/service.py` and `models.py` for actual device data.
The `set_path()` API updates known D-Bus values. Configure MQTT mappings deliberately;
telemetry topics and corresponding `/set` topics influence D-Bus state. Add tests
for operating limits, stale readings, malformed input and unauthorized commands.

## Configuration and MQTT

Copy `config.example.yaml` to `config.yaml`. Environment prefixes `DEVICE_`,
`MQTT_`, `BATTERY_`, `PV_`, `GRID_`, `TANK_` and `TEMP_` override relevant fields.
Use a broker with least-privilege topic ACLs. Credentials require `use_tls: true`;
set the TLS port (usually 8883), and a CA file when using a private authority.
Server hostname and chain verification stay enabled. Certificates must meet
OpenSSL security level 3 (normally RSA >= 3072 bits or ECC >= 256 bits). Optional client certificate
and key must be supplied together. Keep private keys and passwords out of Git.
Plaintext without credentials is only suitable for an isolated trusted network.
Payloads above 64 KiB and invalid UTF-8 are rejected. Generic scaffolding does not
provide device-specific physical safety limits or independent authorization.

## Venus OS installation and removal

First test the exact generated revision on the intended firmware and hardware.
Use firmware Python 3.12 and its D-Bus/GI bindings. Prepare architecture-matching
runtime wheels offline; do not install development dependencies or overwrite
firmware bindings. A virtualenv needs `--system-site-packages` for those bindings.
If firmware lacks venv/ensurepip, prepare a separately tested offline bootstrap.

Copy reviewed source and runtime dependencies to `/data/{{ project_slug }}`.
The following commands perform administrative changes and are not part of tests:

```sh
bash /data/{{ project_slug }}/setup install
svstat /service/{{ project_slug }} /service/{{ project_slug }}/log
tail -n 60 /var/log/{{ project_slug }}/current
bash /data/{{ project_slug }}/setup uninstall
```

The installer uses daemontools, bounded multilog output and a persistent rc.local
hook. Uninstall removes supervision/hooks and retains device-local files. Preserve
configuration and the previous revision before upgrading; rollback restores the
previous tested source/runtime and reruns its installer. Check configuration
compatibility before rollback. Systemd examples apply only to a separate Linux
host; the legacy IPK Makefile is an unsupported migration notice.
